<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Micanopy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1780–<a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Micanopy was a principal chief of the Seminole in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. He was among the leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-seminole-war">Second Seminole War</a>, taking part in the attack on Major Dade's column in December 1835. Captured in 1837, he was removed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>, where he remained a leading chief until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/micanopy.jpg" alt="Micanopy" width="256" height="333" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Micancopy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Micanopy was born near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-augustine-florida">St. Augustine, Florida</a>, around 1780; little else is known of his early years. Before he was chosen as chief he was called Sint-chakkee, &quot;pond frequenter&quot;, and he was also known as Hulbutta Hajo, &quot;Crazy Alligator&quot;. His name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hitchiti">Hitchiti</a> words miko, chief, and naba, above, and means something like &quot;high chief&quot;. He succeeded Bolek as hereditary principal chief on the latter's death in 1819, when he was nearly forty. Position passed through the maternal line among the Seminole.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As chief he acquired large holdings of land and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and, as was common among the Seminole elite, had more than 100 fugitive slaves working his estates. He encouraged intermarriage between Seminole and black people, and some of their descendants rose to influence in the tribal councils. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adams-onis-treaty">Adams–Onís Treaty</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a> became territorial governor in 1821, settlers poured into northern Florida. Micanopy opposed their advance; by the Treaty of Moultrie Creek in 1823 the Americans took 24 million acres of Seminole land, and the Seminole moved south.</p><p>After the Indian Removal Act of 1830, some chiefs signed the Treaty of Payne's Landing on 9 May 1832, giving up more land for a reservation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>. Micanopy refused to sign and joined younger leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osceola">Osceola</a>, Alligator and his nephew Wild Cat in organising resistance.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osceola">Osceola</a> killed the Indian agent Wiley Thompson in December 1835, Micanopy and Osceola attacked the troops of Major Francis Langhorne Dade and General Duncan Lamont Clinch; only three soldiers survived what Americans called Dade's Massacre. Convinced in time that the war could not be won, he surrendered in June 1837 and began to negotiate removal, but Osceola carried him off. He was later seized by the forces of General Thomas S. Jesup under a flag of truce, a breach that turned much public feeling toward the Seminole.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After imprisonment in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> he was sent west with about 200 other Seminoles, who were at first placed under the authority of the Creek Nation. He never regained his former power, but in 1845 he signed a treaty giving the Seminole partial independence from the Creek Nation, with full independence promised for 1855. He died at Fort Gibson on 2 January 1849 and was succeeded by his sister's son, Jim Jumper. The town of Micanopy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, stands on the site of his capital, Cuscowilla, and is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Micanopy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Second Seminole War of 1835 to 1842, led by Osceola and <b>Micanopy</b>, the Seminole fought the United States to a costly standstill, but most were removed to Indian Territory, where Wewoka became their capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/seminole-nation">Seminole Nation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-augustine-florida">St. Augustine</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Fort Gibson, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.9,-81.31&amp;year=1824" aria-label="St. Augustine on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2436 1072 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Micanopy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2886 1342h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2886 1342h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.9,-81.31&amp;year=1824">What was here in 1824? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
